The missing-deliverable fixture: a scratch root with no `doctor-gate-evidence.md` gives `--part b` exit 1 and cases_total=12 cases_failed=12. Each case names the absent record. The check does not pass on a missing deliverable.

### A209, the leaked stub message

`case_tsx_exit_1_gate_exits_0` exports `DOCTOR_GATE_TSX_MESSAGE` at line 198. `run_case` does not unset it at line 1064, and `tsx_exit_0_gate_exits_0` follows in the run order. Its stub therefore prints the verdict line from the earlier case. The case comment says it proves that an exit 0 with no verdict line still exits 0; the fixture no longer holds that input.

Proof: mutant p3-20 survives on the phase head (cases_total=19 cases_failed=0). At the phase base the same mutant dies (cases_total=13 cases_failed=2), so Phase 3 caused the loss. Adding `reset_tsx` at the start of the case kills the mutant again (cases_total=19 cases_failed=1).

Effect: the production exit 0 rule is correct, but section 7.4 of test-strategy.md claims a p3-20 kill it does not have. The P3 adversarial run will report one survivor. Severity: Minor, nonblocking. The fix is one test line, and it belongs to the Red side before the adversarial run.

### A210, the single positive fixture

`VERDICT_LINE` at line 23 is the only valid verdict fixture. The seven malformed shapes and the two longer-line fixtures all sit around that one count set. A mutant pattern that hard-codes `debt-change (files=12, findings=3, parseErrors=0)` passes all 19 cases. A second valid line with different counts would kill it. Severity: Nit.

